Standard Dolby Vision through the NUC7's MegaChips MCDP2800 LSPCON, switched
to level-shifter mode (gen9/10).

In PCON mode the MCDP2800 (FW 1.77) forwards the HDR10 SDP but drops the
Dolby VSIF. In level-shifter mode it repeats the GPU's own TMDS, so the
native-HDMI DV path (DV AVI, Dolby VSIF) reaches the TV. With
DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB set, the DP connector's DDI computes an HDMI-mode
output (intel_ddi_compute_output_type()); the HDMI enable path switches the
LSPCON to level-shifter mode through the standard dual-mode register and the
disable path switches it back to PCON mode. Without DV the port is the usual
PCON-mode DP connector (4K60, HDR10). Level-shifter mode stops at 340 Mchar/s
and gen9 HDMI at 300 MHz: DV is 4K up to 30 Hz.

While in level-shifter mode the LSPCON does not answer native AUX: DP
detect reports HPD only and keeps the EDID, short HPD pulses and DP link
checks are skipped, and intel_lspcon_resume() leaves the chip alone. QMS is
off on this route (the VTEM does not fit gen9's 32-byte GMP DIP), and only
the Dolby VSIF tunnel format is allowed (no DRM InfoFrame DIP on gen9).

Eligibility: i915.dv_lspcon (-1 = DMI-allowlisted boards [NUC7i7BNB], 0 = off,
1 = any gen9/10 LSPCON port), and at modeset a MegaChips MC2800 with an
allowlisted firmware (1.77).

Verified with the NUC Display Lab (nuc-hardware-lab v0.3.1/v0.4.0-dvlab,
reports #15 and #17): NUC7i7BNB, MCDP2800 HW 2.2 FW 1.77, LG OLED55C3,
4K23.976 RGB8; DV picture modes, test bars from the RGB8 tunnel, no-VSIF
control shows noise, and a metadata-only A/B pair renders darker/brighter.
That lab switched modes by rebinding i915; the in-modeset switch of this
patch is not yet hardware-verified. Applies after linux-9930.

